 analytical method it's going to help  provide  important surveillance information for  public health agencies  sometimes timely data collection in an  outbreak can have a significant  impact in supporting public policy or  allowing points of intervention to be  identified  and sometimes with some of these opioid  drugs  you might get an outbreak so for example  in one particular location there's a bad  batch of drugs that contains  a very strong opioid or a new opioid  that nobody's seen before  and if a forensic lab can help identify  the drugs  and alert public health they can uh give  out  warnings to people in the drug community  um and they could at least be aware that  there's an issue with  some of the supply of drugs um a lot of  areas  they have naloxone kits available um  but if people know that there's an issue  with the drug supply  it might encourage them to have these  uh additional kind of intervention ready  to go  if there's issues  so with the um appearance of  the some of these novel psychoactive  substances  um and in particular the opioids in  terms of the opioid crisis  some of the challenges are that  compounds can appear and disappear  very rapidly so a new drug might appear  one month  and then two or three months later it  might be gone from the market  so laboratories need to be able to adapt  quickly  for many uh forensic toxicology  laboratories  with limited resources this can be a  real issue  so some um forensic labs they're  publicly funded  they're able to do kind of routine  testing and they can do it quite well  but if you get new drugs that need to be  tested  you might have to decide well where are  we going to put our resources  which drugs are going to be more  prevalent  and also which drugs are going to be  more toxic  could should we focus on those first  an example of that would be the uh the  first appearance of car fentanyl so  in canada it first appeared on the drug  scene  in around 2016 and that was similar to  the united states now car fentanyl  is a um related to fentanyl so it's a  fentanyl analog  it's approved for use in large animals  but it has no approved use in humans  and in terms of potency carfentanil  is a hundred times stronger than  fentanyl  and it's ten thousand times stronger  than morphine  so you could see just looking at the  strength of carfentanil  how it could be a real issue if people  are  starting to take drugs that contain  carfentanil  so for forensic labs it created  analytical challenges  because carfentanil appears in in the  drug supply  and a lab needs to be able to adapt to  that and be able to  test biological samples to see if  carfentanil is present  and if so how much is present so  you need to be able to establish a  sensitive method  so you can quantitate carfentanil and  when it's a drug such as carfentanil  that is so  potent you need to have a method that's  capable of detecting very very low  concentrations  and that can be a challenge and it can  take time to set up a method so you can  see that also even just something as  simple as obtaining analytical standards  for method development can be difficult  there's a lot of restrictions on being  able to get  car fentanyl standards that you need to  develop a method  so sometimes it can take months just to  get the  the legal ability to buy car fentanyl  standards for the laboratory to be able  to  develop these methods and when it takes  months to develop these methods  that people are still kind of dying of  overdoses  that creates a real challenge for a  toxicology lab  because you want to be able to keep up  to date and be able to assist  investigators in what drugs are  currently being used  another example of this is the  uh novel psychoactive opioid  u47700  now similar to car fentanyl this first  appeared in canada  and again similar in the united states  in around 2016.  um this drug the users of this drug  report that  it's more euphoric than fentanyl so some  of the drug using communities  they like this drug it was going to be a  popular drug  even though we call it a novel  psychoactive substance  it was actually first developed in 1978  by a pharmaceutical company  but it never actually progressed and was  used in humans so it first appeared so  in canada for example i think it was  approximately five deaths were first  reported in 2016  early 2017 and then there was a change  in the legal status and it became a  scheduled  one-control substance and this change in  the legal status  meant that the popularity of u477  uh zero zero like just immediately  uh dropped and actually  users then switched to a similar drug  zero u49900 um it's an analog  four seven seven zero zero so it's  um it's structurally uh similar  but this wasn't uh made by a pharmacy  pharmaceutical company  so there was absolutely no published  literature about this drug  so it was a brand new chemical so  toxicology labs had to develop  methods for the detection of this drug  there's no information available  about toxic concentrations potentially  fatal concentrations  and it's an example of how things change  very very quickly  just because of a scheduling change so  in canada and in the united states when  both  the government set legal restrictions on  you four seven seven  zero zero the drug community immediately  switched to another chemical  so it's that plain catch-up that  forensic toxicology labs  uh have to do  so how do forensic toxicology labs can  monitor these drug trends that are going  on  because you can get trends in countries  but even within countries in different  regional areas one drug might be popular  for a few  months and then change and then in a  different  area a different drug might become  popular so again  it's that adapt like forensic labs have  to adapt to  all these changes so it's very important  for labs to try to  to monitor what's going on so how does  forensic toxicologist do that  well drug seizures can be a great source  of information  so um sometimes drugs are seized  at the border they can get tested police  investigations maybe if they seize drugs  in a drug trafficking investigation  if that drug is tested the information  from those drug seizures provides  information about what's currently out  on the streets  another way is testing in forensic  laboratories or testing in clinical  laboratories  sometimes if labs identify a new drug  they will publish the method they'll  alert the community and say  hey this is this is a new compound that  we've just found in this  area um a good way of keeping up to date  with information  is the society of forensic toxicologists  so their website has information on a  lot of the new  novel psychoactive opioids and other  drugs too  published case reports can be a good way  of getting information  although by the time a case report is  published it can be  you know at least two or three months  out a day and because the drug trends  change so quickly  um it might already be out of date but  it's still good to know  kind of what's out there so for example  a published case report might be  a couple of deaths that have occurred  due to some  novel opioid that's appeared  and then an additional source of  information is actually some of the  websites that drug users use  so they will post information about  drugs what they're currently using what  the effects are  they'll compare like the strength of the  drugs to like other drugs that are  already on the market some examples of  that would be error word  reddit or a website called psychonaut  wiki  so forensic toxicologists sometimes find  it useful to monitor some of these drug  user websites  because it does provide some good  information  some other useful resources that are  really good  is in the united states the drug  enforcement administration  and the national forensic laboratory  information system  they both provide information on  drugs that are detected in drug testing  labs and forensic laboratories  all across the united states and that  information is available on their  website  so it's a great way of keeping up to  date with what drugs  are currently being seen  and then in europe they have a european  monitoring center for drugs  and drug addiction and they have a  really good  early warning system on new psychoactive  substances  so they'll provide information about  what drugs that are currently being  seen in europe and what some of the  effects of the drugs are  like if there's any deaths being  reported so again  it provides a lot of useful information  and it's really important for forensic  toxicologists to try and keep up to date  with some of the changes that are  occurring  um because there's so many challenges  with being able to  keep up with what's being used out in  the  out in the real world  okay so thank you for listening um i  provided the overview about challenges  for forensic toxicologists in relation  to the opioid crisis  um and if you have any follow-up  questions please feel free to email me  my email address is karen.woodall at  utoronto.ca  so thanks very much
 

